  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1105 AM MDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nLow pressure will continue shift northeast across Utah, Idaho,\nand Wyoming through tonight. Stronger showers and storms will\nproduce heavier downpours, with the potential for 0.10\"-0.20\" in\nthose situations or where multiple showers/storms move over the\nsame location. The potential for anything over 0.20\" is 30-60%,\nbut that is not widespread and fits with the idea of\nshowers/storms vs widespread precipitation. There isn't too \nmuch potential for gusty outflow winds, generally 10% or less, \nexcept around Bear Lake where the potential is pushing 50% \nthrough midnight. \n\nWe go back to dry and warmer conditions through Friday morning.\nHighs quickly bounce back into the 70s with some lower 80s mixed\nin Thursday and Friday. Friday into Saturday, low pressure\nswings by to our northwest. We should see some light rain showers\nand storms across the central mountains, but the main hazard \nwill be stronger winds across all of central and eastern Idaho. \nGusts of 20-35 mph will be common across the entire area, with \nsome gusts around 40 mph possible across the Arco Desert, \ncentral mountains and along the Montana border. Saturday and\nbeyond is a mess forecast-wise. The ECMWF continues to show a\nweaker low crossing Idaho early next week. The GFS wants to\ndevelop split flow and drag the low farther south. The caveat\nwith the latest version of that model is that it's still far\nenough north to keep showers and storms in the forecast, AND\nalso wants to clip the state with deeper tropical moisture from\nthe Pacific. This would be coming from Hurricane Polo IF this\npattern pans out. Needless to say, there potential forecast\nideas is pretty wide ranging. The Blend of Models shows a\ngradual increase in showers and storms during that timeframe,\nwhich seems perfectly reasonable. Temperatures after Friday\nremain cooler, with valley highs in the 60s and lower 70s.",
